From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7993: open the download window with the command that opens it

Skipping the ROM patch answers the question the last four rounds were asking
around.  With no PATCH_FINISH_REQ, no endpoint reset, no recovery and nothing
restored afterwards, the RAM code still goes into a ring the chip never
fetches:

  diag cmd push: len=76 head=1 tail=0 didx=0
  diag cmd done: tail=1 head=1 didx=1        <- TARGET_ADDRESS_LEN_REQ answered
  diag fwdl push: len=4032 head=1  didx=0 base=0x8d4f0000 cnt=0x300 cidx=0x01
  diag fwdl push: len=2128 head=37 didx=0 ... cidx=0x25

So the ring is not disabled, not paused, not short of credits - outside the
ring the flow control bypass is already on, set by the chip itself when it
enters download mode (GLO_CFG_EXT1 reads 0x9c800404 without anyone asking).
The one thing that differs between the download that moves and the one that
does not is the command in front of it: the patch is asked for with
PATCH_START_REQ and its payload is fetched, the RAM code is asked for with
TARGET_ADDRESS_LEN_REQ and its payload is not.

In a flow where the endpoint survives the jump, the window the ROM opened for
the patch simply stays open into the RAM download, which is why the vendor
never has to think about it.  Here it does not survive, so the window has to
be opened again - with the same command.

  diag_ram_as_patch=1  send the RAM download with PATCH_START_REQ, carrying
		       the region's own address, length and mode;

  diag_ram_sem=1       the same idea one step earlier: take and release the
		       patch semaphore around the RAM download, in case that
		       is what the receiver waits for.

Both default to off, so the untouched build behaves like 053.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
@@ -37,6 +37,28 @@
 #include "hw_ops.h"
 #include "mac_if.h"
 
+/*
+ * The download ring only ever moves for the first download of a boot, the one
+ * that follows PATCH_START_REQ - and skipping the patch proves that this has
+ * nothing to do with the endpoint leaving the bus: with the ROM left alone and
+ * no reset anywhere, the RAM code still goes into a ring the chip never
+ * fetches, while TARGET_ADDRESS_LEN_REQ is answered normally.
+ *
+ * So the ring is not short of credits and not disabled; the receiver simply
+ * only opens its window when PATCH_START_REQ arrives.  In a flow without the
+ * reset that window stays open from the patch download into the RAM download,
+ * which is why the vendor never notices.  Ask for the RAM code with the same
+ * command that opens it, and it should be able to close again afterwards.
+ */
+static bool diag_ram_as_patch;
+module_param(diag_ram_as_patch, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_ram_as_patch, "open the download window with PATCH_START_REQ for the RAM code");
+
+/* Same idea, one step earlier: re-acquire the semaphore the ROM watched. */
+static bool diag_ram_sem;
+module_param(diag_ram_sem, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_ram_sem, "acquire and release the patch semaphore around the RAM download");
+
 static int
 mcu_wm_patch_sem_ctrl(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev, bool get)
 {
@@ -192,7 +214,9 @@
 		mcu_txblk.cmd = MCU_CMD_PATCH_START_REQ;
 		break;
 	case MCU_FWDL_TYPE_RAM:
-		mcu_txblk.cmd = MCU_CMD_TARGET_ADDRESS_LEN_REQ;
+		mcu_txblk.cmd = diag_ram_as_patch ?
+				MCU_CMD_PATCH_START_REQ :
+				MCU_CMD_TARGET_ADDRESS_LEN_REQ;
 		break;
 	default:
 		return -EINVAL;
@@ -738,6 +762,10 @@
 
 	u32 reg_start, reg_end, len_cnt = 0;
 
+	if (diag_ram_sem)
+		dev_err(dev->dev, "diag: ram sem get -> %d\n",
+			mcu_wm_patch_sem_ctrl(dev, 1));
+
 	for (i = 0; i < hdr->n_region; i++) {
 		const struct mcu_fw_region *region;
 		u32 len = 0, addr = 0, mode = 0;
@@ -829,6 +857,10 @@
 	if (is_wa)
 		option |= FW_START_WORKING_PDA_CR4;
 
+	if (diag_ram_sem)
+		dev_err(dev->dev, "diag: ram sem release -> %d\n",
+			mcu_wm_patch_sem_ctrl(dev, 0));
+
 	mcu_diag_chip(dev, "pre-start");
 
 	return mcu_wm_start_firmware(dev, override, option);
